From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: DrummingBird1 <1979036+DrummingBird1@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Tue, 3 Nov 2026 14:52:11 +0200
Subject: [PATCH] GCM/D3D12: read the FIFO ahead and compile new vertex
 programs on workers

A title uploads each draw's vertex program right before the draw, so no
program reaches the runtime earlier than the draw that needs it, and the
shaders new at a scene cut were compiled one after another as the walker
reached them: four in Sonic the Hedgehog's story scene, 257 ms in a row.
But the title flushes a whole frame at once.

At the start of each drain pass, gcm_vp_prefetch_scan reads from get to
put with a shadow of the microcode store, follows JUMPs and one-deep
CALLs as the walker does (Sonic builds every draw, its program upload
included, in a buffer of its own that the ring CALLs), and offers each
program a draw will run to g_rsx_vp_prefetch. The D3D12 backend decompiles
a new one and compiles it on the system thread pool (shader_prefetch);
shader_compile then waits for that job instead of compiling it again, and
the worker writes the shader cache. Read only: the walk is unchanged.
D3D12_SHADER_PREFETCH=0 turns it off; GCM_PREFETCH_DBG=1 reports what the
read-ahead covered.

Story scene, first run (empty cache), alternating: the cut's stall
277-287 ms -> 161-166 ms (18-20 -> 10 vblanks), 29.81 -> 29.90 flips/s;
34 vertex programs from workers. Fragment programs are not read ahead.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
